Date: Tue, 23 Nov 93 11:43:22 PST
From: tcmay@netcom.com (Timothy C. May)
Subject: Comments on NSA (was: "Pyrrhus Cracks RSA?") (fwd)

Extropiates,

Forgive me for forwarding an article I just posted to the Cypherpunks
list, as many of you are on both lists. But the issue of breaking PGP
and other public key encryption systems has come up here on this list,
as it has on FIDONet, and my article addresses this issue.

Specifically, Harvey Newstrom asks:

"Exactly.  PGP and other crypto methods are based on the time it takes
to perform calculations.  A magnitude increase in computer technology
will make craking PGP easier.  In the very-long-term, when computer arrays,
or time machines become possible, all messages are almost guaranteed to
be cracked.  How far in the futre does it have to be until you don't
care?"
     
The short answer: The computer power required to brute-force crack RSA
(via factoring the modulus) grows exponentially with the key length. A
150-digit number (corresponding roughly to a 512-bit key, a very small
one by PGP standards) is beyond our ability to factor using all the
computers in the world running for a few years. In ten years, this
will still be the case--probably.

A 1024-bit key should be impregnable for several decades, even given
specialized hardware and modest improvements in factoring algorithms,
A 1500-bit key should be feasible to use with Pentium-class machines
and will be uncrackable for centuries, perhaps millenia.

(Rivest had a nice presentation at the last RSA conference, with a
table of key sizes and Cray-years needed to break them.)

But here are some comments I just made on this issue, especially as it
relates to speculations that the NSA is either overly clever ("they
have methods!") or overly stupid ("just a bunch of government
bureaucrats").

--Tim

