>The other insight I have is that you probably haven't solved
>the most vexing problem of information retrieval, which I will
>losely term "indexing".  Your system may start out easy to use,
>when the client can send someone in the front office a message
>about what their problem is and the front office goes "oh yes.
>That's Tim May's area.  I'll forward your query to him and he
>will be in touch."  But systems like that don't scale up.  They
>work well when there is a simple algorithm to identify where to 
>look for the info, or when the infobase is small enough for one
>or two human minds to have a good intuitive feel for where things
>should be.  Once you have a whole bunch of people participating,
>and the questions get less than clear, it will get hard to figure
>out who your clients should talk to.  

>At this point, your mind should be roiling with cool high-tech
>ways to match clients with consultants.  Good.  But talk to
>people in the field before you sink money into it.  Some wheels
>have been invented and broken already, others have been invented
>but never really tested.

A real breakthrough in the indexing of human knowledge would have
astonishing impact on human society. Imagine if you could ask a
machine, or even a human augmented with machines, "has anyone ever
started a company to do X before?" or "has anyone ever built anything
like my new invention" or "has this area of mathematics been gone over
before?" or "how to I construct a frazimatan" and have a coherent
answer spit out along with enough cross references that you could
understand it. Perhaps others don't see what I'm getting at, but from
what I can tell such a system would have amazing implications. So much
of what people do is sell access to specialized information -- what if
it was all easy to get at?

>From what I can tell, really good indexing has yet to be developed.
Indeed, in some ways indexing is like a superset of the AI problem --
you need to be able not only to understand new information but to be
able to see all its implications everywhere in order ot cross index it
properly. I thus expect that superindexing is unlikely to show up any
time soon.

However, I will point out that things like the Library of Congress and
other systems for systematically classifying all human knowledge do
exist and have been worked on very hard by the professionals in this
field. The professionals in this field already exist: they are called
LIBRARIANS. Most people think of librarians as being the old women who
sit around and answer small children's questions on how to use card
catalogues -- few people realize that what librarians actually worry
about is this very problem -- the indexing of all human knowledge.


Perry

