In #93-11-879, nancy@genie.slhs.udel.edu asks a good question:
|> Would encrypted money make fractional reserve banking 
(a) impossible,

This gets into implementation, I think. If the bank is also the
issuing agent and trusted mediator, as in most schemes I've read
about, then they can accept deposits in whatever form, convert them to
bank-bucks of another form, invest those, and reissue/convert
depositor's digicash as they spend it. Since digicash is secure
anyway, the safekeeping function of the bank is not needed in the same
way that vaults are needed to protect notes and bullion today.
[I should probably go reread Chaum or the relevant chapter of Schneier
before sending this, as I have a nagging feeling I'm missing something]

(b) clearly fraudulent,
That's the nice thing about cryptographic protocols, you can design
the fraud out. Obviously if a bank was known to use a corruptible
protocol they'd be asking to be wiped out.

(c) only possible as a well-defined gamble, or

That's what it is now. Banks usually arrange short-term lending pools
at reasonable rates to ensure liquidity. Or governments step in and
create monsters like the Fed to make sure they do it.

(d) other?

I can see two ways a digital bank could make a profit -- transaction
fees and fractional reserve arrangements permitting the bank to make
loans and investments. The former could be almost completely
automated; your bank / issuer could be very little above and beyond a
present day ATM network in complexity.

-- Craig

