> What I wonder about, and am unknowledgable about, is the use of frequency
> analysis on PGP and other related encryption schemes... finding patterns
> in masses of archived pgp encrypted mail --encrypted in a specific key.

By "brute force" I meant factoring of the modulus, not trying every
possible key. Factoring the modulus, the large number n that is the
product of two primes p and q, allows the immediate reconstruction of
the secret key (by some math related to the RSA algorithm).

It is not yet proven if access to plaintext-ciphertext pairs "leaks"
information and makes breaking RSA easier than factoring the modulus.

> Could it be conceivably possible that cryptanalysis ten years from now
> could be able to decrypt the letters we are sending today, much
> more efficiently and timely than brute force?

Sure, this is "conceivable." How _likely_ it is depends on a number of
things and is partly a matter of judgement.

> Moreover, could this cryptanalysis software running on much faster computers
> (possibly massivily parellel), be quick enough at recognizing patterns in
> readily available quantities of encrypted material (years of archived
> postings)?

Don't overestimate the role of "massively parallel" computers. For a
lot of problems, a 1000-fold speedup is really nice to have, but for
other problems it makes little difference on a practical scale.
Cutting the time to factor a number from 10^10 years to 10^7 years is
not a big deal.

> I suppose changing keys frequently takes on new importance...
> 

Editorial remarks:

This is another reason to oppose the government-sanctioned approaches
to encryption. The schemes they like make changing keys very
difficult, requiring registration and (probably) payment of fees. 

And generating dozens or even thousands of keys for use in various
transactions is truly scary to them.


--Tim May

